Z||000002||N_Man||03-13-2001||10:24 PM||||hehe<BR>if your into expensive stuff that always breaks then why not try it...<P>Actually my dad had one of those a few years back, it spent much more time broken than it did running.  About a month after he got it the engine melted a piston, so we rebuilt the engine with the usual go fast stuff, increased C/R, ported heads, better carb...  Well after that it was the FASTEST damn car ive ever driven, if it was having a good day other times it felt really sluggish...<BR>then the driveshaft broke... rear end developed a bad clunk...  Ummm its been a few years, so i cant remember all of it but the car was SOOOO unreliable matched with getting parts from overseas (worst case, but there were a few parts direct from italy).<P>I guess what im getting at is unless you absolutly have to try it (my guess is a slim chance of it working if any chance at all)  STAY AWAY...<P>But in some ways i wish my dad kept that car it was so fast and most people didnt know what the thing was.||||reg||4
Z||000003||862M4inCA||03-13-2001||11:20 PM||||I'm not sure if this is the same motor that I heard horror stories about (tho I think ANY Maserati engine would be similar) those cars....My Dad used to work for a parts supplier and knew someone who owned a maserati and needed a set of plug wires (cheap right??? NOT) The guy had to drop $500 for a set and 2 weeks delivery wait just for PLUG WIRES....I think I'd rather take my chances with attempting to install a jet engine instead. hehe||||reg||1
Z||000004||PJ||03-14-2001||12:30 AM||||N_Man is right. It will break your heart and then your bank account.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rodmcneill||03-14-2001||01:01 AM||||Anyone who would pay $500.00 for plug wires has to be nuts. Most of the bigger speed shops sell bulk spark plug wires, ends and boots. Various colors and diameters available and probably higher grade wires than the originals. I run Taylor wires on both of my GT's and have been very pleased. Making up your wires for length is very simple.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-14-2001||01:06 AM||||I learned to drive in a Biturbo.  The car was brand new at the time.<P>Every 2K miles, the intake manifold gaskets would fail.  The Biturbo uses a carburetor with a turbo . . . can we say "carb in a box"?<P>While it was a sweet runner, I would not want one in my driveway.  At the time, the Merkur XR4ti was almost as fast and much more reliable.<P>If only a Ford 2.3L intercooled turbo would bolt into my Fiero.  The 2.3L ford engine has an overhead cam and it known to rev very high.  Unlike Fiero engines, the 2.3L is an easy engine to tune up at a low cost.  I have a Racer Walsh catalog and the SVT parts are very trick.<P>I am always thinking about putting a turbo on my 4cylinder Ford Ranger truck.  But I guess that's getting off topic.<P>Skip the Maz' motor.  It will be expensive, difficult, and broken most of the time.  That's the reality of the Biturbo.  Think "nice place to visit, but I wouldn't want to live there."||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cozmo||03-14-2001||07:41 AM||||The reason I asked is when picking up another Fiero yesterday I looked over a '85 Maz' with 27K, original tires and all there.<BR>Seems that 10 years ago the PO's girlfriend in a jealous rage cut the whole wiring harness in the engine compartment and it hasn't run since.  The engine sure is sweet looking, the turbos look about the right size for a Fiero also. The guy wants much less than a grand for it. I won't do it, but sometimes I get crazy ideas <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>C02M0||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||03-14-2001||08:13 AM||||he'll be ok.. he close to Prestige Imports... one of 2 Maserati parts importers in the US..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   250hp and 260lbft.. OH YEAH!||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||03-14-2001||09:58 AM||||I wouldnt. May be nice power, but a real pain in the azz for parts and service. I dont even know if they sell Maserati in US anymore......or even make them at all.||||reg||1
